module

public import LakeJs.RecSchema
public import LakeJs.TyData
public import LakeJs.JSNumberNat
public import LakeJs.JSBigInt
public import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic
public import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Lattice.Basic
public import LakeJs.NonEmptyString

@[expose] public section


/-!
# `Expr n js g` — a **total** (strongly normalizing) JavaScript expression language

An `Expr n js g` is a JavaScript expression with `n` free de Bruijn holes, all of whose
opaque names are declared in the finite set `g`.  Two things are built into the type.

**Termination.** The language has no first-class functions and no application, and
instead two structural eliminators:

* `Expr.foldEnum` — a **catamorphism** over a compiled Lean inductive value.  The
  family is a `LeanRecFamily`, i.e. a schema that remembers which constructor fields
  are recursive occurrences, so the fold walks a finite tree: one branch per
  constructor, whose body receives the constructor's fields with every *recursive*
  field already replaced by the result of folding it.
* `Expr.foldArray` — `Array.prototype.reduce` / `reduceRight` over a JavaScript
  array, which is finite too.

Binders occur **only** as the branch bodies of these two nodes; there is no node for
"a function" and none for "apply".  An `Expr` therefore cannot express a loop, cannot
diverge, and cannot throw: `LakeJs.Run` gives it a total interpreter and `LakeJs.Eval`
a total partial evaluator — no fuel anywhere, and no `partial def`.

**The opaque names are in the type.**  The only constructor that names one,

```lean
| unsafeGlobal (name : NEString) (mem : name ∈ g) : Expr n js g
```

demands a proof that the name is declared, so an `Expr n js ∅` cannot mention an opaque
name at all: there is no proof of `name ∈ ∅` to supply.  The bound is not a side
condition to be re-established after every pass — every operation of this development
has type `Expr n js g → Expr n js g`, so "the optimizer never invents an opaque name" is a
typing judgement (see `LakeJs.Globals`).  The index is uniform: every subterm of an
`Expr n js g` is over the same `g`, so `g` is an upper bound for the whole tree rather
than the exact set used at each node, and `Expr.mono` widens it.

Everything an `Expr` can express can be compiled to `while`-based JavaScript: see
`LakeJs.Render`, which renders a fold over a *linear* family (a list, a snoc-list, a
chain of nodes) as a `while` loop over an explicit stack rather than as a recursive
function.

## The one non-fold binder: `letIn`

`Expr.letIn rhs body` names a value.  It adds no shape of recursion — `body` is a
strict subterm — so nothing about termination changes; what it does touch is *every*
traversal of `Expr`.  The four decisions it was added under:

* **`BExpr` has no binder.**  A boolean-valued binding is
  `BExpr.truthy (Expr.letIn …)`.  Consequence: a `let` cannot be floated out of a
  `BExpr` condition without going through `Expr`.
* **The engine keeps a binding only when the right-hand side is residual.**  If the
  right-hand side evaluates to a *value* it is inlined — exactly as a known array's
  elements are inlined into a fold's body — which is what keeps
  `Expr.evalX_isValue` (`LakeJs.EngineValue`) true.  `Expr.isValue` is therefore
  `false` on a `letIn`.
* **Surface syntax** (`LakeJs.Elab`): the immediately invoked arrow
  `((x) => body)(rhs)`, which is what `Expr.renderAux` emits so that rendering round
  trips, with `let x = rhs in body` as sugar.
* **Common-subexpression elimination is a pass of its own**, not a rule of the
  engine: a rule that makes a term *bigger* would have to be reconciled with
  `Expr.evalX_isNormal`, and it would loop against the inline-a-variable-used-once
  rule.

A new constructor of `Expr` breaks the case list of every function and theorem that
enumerates them.  The map, as `letIn` found it:

| file | declarations that grow a case |
| --- | --- |
| `Expr.lean` | `Expr.mono`, `Expr.size`, `Expr.mentions`, `Expr.occurrences` |
| `Subst.lean`, `Stage.lean` | `Expr.rename`, `rename_congr`, `rename_rename` |
| `Run.lean`, `RunStrict.lean`, `RunRefine.lean` | `Expr.run`, `Expr.runS`, their agreement |
| `RunRename.lean`, `PreserveRename.lean` | `run_rename`, `runS_rename` |
| `Eval.lean`, `EvalCore.lean` | `Expr.evalX`, `Expr.check`, `Expr.checkTy` |
| `EngineValueBasic.lean`, `EngineValue.lean`, `EngineNormal.lean` | `noOpaque`, `isValue`, `evalX_isValue`, `evalX_id_of_isValue` |
| `RenameEngine.lean`, `RenameVal.lean` | `evalX_rename`, `checkTy_rename`, `isValue_rename`, `jsStr?_rename` |
| `Globals.lean`, `JSVal.lean` | `noOpaque_of_empty`, `jsBigint?` |
| `Normal.lean`, `NormalBasic.lean`, `Idempotent.lean` | `IsNormal`, `IsNormal.rename`, `evalX_isNormal` |
| `Rewrite.lean`, `Terminating.lean`, `Diamond.lean` | `OptStep`, `redexCount`, `optStep_diamond` |
| `Render.lean`, `RenderInvariants.lean` | `renderAux`, `NumsPrintable`, `renderAux_congr` |
| `TyCheck.lean`, `TySound.lean`, `TyMono.lean`, `TyRename.lean`, `TyOptStep.lean`, `TySimp.lean`, `TyMigrate.lean` | `inferTy` and its laws |
| `TyFold*.lean`, `CheckTySound.lean`, `Deep/*.lean` | `isTreeExpr`, `inferTyT`, `checkTy_sound`, `inferDTy` |
| `Elab.lean` | the quotation |

## Conventions

* Variables are de Bruijn *indices* into `Fin n`.  The parameters of a binder occupy
  the **last** indices: in a body of type `Expr (n + p) js g` the parameter `j` is
  `#(n + j)`, and the enclosing variables keep their indices.
* Container types (`ExprList`, `ObjProps`, `ExprVec`, `Branches`) are bespoke members
  of the same `mutual` block, because `List (Expr n js g)` would be a nested inductive
  whose parameters mention the local `n` and `g` (leanprover/lean4#1964).
* Everything statically known to be a boolean is a `BExpr`.
* Strings that must be printable to make sense — the name of an `unsafeGlobal`, a
  property name, the key of an object literal, the source text of a numeric literal,
  the tag tested by `isTag` — are `NEString` (`NonEmptyString`), so `""` is rejected by
  the type checker rather than silently producing `x.` or `{: 1}`.  An `Expr.str`
  literal keeps the type `String`: `""` is a perfectly good JavaScript string.
-/

/-- Which end an array fold starts from. -/
inductive FoldDir where
  /-- `Array.prototype.reduceRight`: from the last element towards the first. -/
  | right
  /-- `Array.prototype.reduce`: from the first element towards the last. -/
  | left
  deriving Repr, DecidableEq, BEq, Inhabited

/-- Binary operators of `Expr` that return a boolean.

    Besides the JavaScript operators this also holds the **boolean-valued primitive
    methods** — `s.includes(t)`, `s.startsWith(t)`, `s.endsWith(t)`.  A method belongs
    here rather than in a node of its own because its meaning is a total function of
    the two values it is applied to, which is exactly the criterion of `METHODS.md`
    §1: adding one costs `Expr` no property, and adds no new shape of recursion. -/
inductive BinOpBool where
  | eq | strictEq | neq | strictNeq | lt | le | gt | ge
  /-- `s.includes(t)` for strings. -/
  | strIncludes
  /-- `s.startsWith(t)`. -/
  | strStartsWith
  /-- `s.endsWith(t)`. -/
  | strEndsWith
  deriving Repr, DecidableEq, BEq, Inhabited

/-- Binary operators of `Expr` that do not return a boolean — and the **binary
    primitive methods**, `s.split(t)`, `s.indexOf(t)`, `s.slice(k)`, `s.charAt(k)`.
    See `BinOpBool` for why a method may live in an operator table. -/
inductive BinOp where
  | plus | minus | times | divide | mod
  /-- `a ** b`, exponentiation.  Right-associative in JavaScript, and binding tighter
      than `*`.  The engine folds it only where the exact model has an answer — an
      integer exponent within `JSNumber.powExpBound`, or a non-negative bigint
      exponent — and leaves it residual otherwise: `2 ** 0.5` is legal JavaScript this
      backend does not evaluate. -/
  | pow
  | bitAnd | bitOr | bitXor | lsh | rsh | ursh
  /-- `Math.min(a, b)` and `Math.max(a, b)`, on two arguments.  Exact on this model:
      `NaN` wins over everything, and the two zeros are ordered, `-0 < +0`. -/
  | mathMin | mathMax
  /-- `a ?? b`: `b` when `a` is `null` or `undefined`, `a` otherwise.  Short-circuit
      in JavaScript, which is invisible here: an `Expr` is pure and total, so
      evaluating `b` for nothing can neither be observed nor diverge. -/
  | nullish
  /-- `s.split(sep)` for a string separator. -/
  | strSplit
  /-- `s.indexOf(t)`, `-1` when absent. -/
  | strIndexOf
  /-- `s.lastIndexOf(t)`, `-1` when absent. -/
  | strLastIndexOf
  /-- `s.slice(k)`; a negative `k` counts from the end. -/
  | strSliceFrom
  /-- `s.charAt(k)`, the empty string when out of range. -/
  | strCharAt
  /-- `s.at(k)`; a negative `k` counts from the end, out of range is `undefined`. -/
  | strAt
  /-- `s.codePointAt(k)`: the code point of the character at `k`, `undefined` out of
      range.  This is the node `EXPR-DROP-LAM-CALL-PLAN.md` §2.5 asks for: the code
      point of a `Char` used to be hidden from the optimizer behind an immediately
      invoked arrow, which is a use of a function value to fake a missing operation.
      With the operation here, a known character folds and an unknown one stays
      residual, which is exactly what the arrow was imitating. -/
  | strCodePointAt
  deriving Repr, DecidableEq, BEq, Inhabited

/-- Unary operators of `Expr` that do not return a boolean — and the **unary primitive
    methods**: `String(x)`, `s.toUpperCase()`, `s.toLowerCase()`, `s.trim()`,
    `Object.keys/values/entries(o)`. -/
inductive UnOp where
  | plus | minus | tilde | typeof
  /-- `Math.floor(x)`, `Math.ceil(x)`, `Math.trunc(x)`, `Math.round(x)`,
      `Math.abs(x)` and `Math.sign(x)`.  These are the `Math` functions whose value is
      a rational whenever the argument is one, so the exact model computes them; the
      irrational ones (`sqrt`, `exp`, the trigonometric functions) are excluded, see
      `METHODS.md`. -/
  | mathFloor | mathCeil | mathTrunc | mathRound | mathAbs | mathSign
  /-- `void x`: `undefined`, whatever `x` is. -/
  | voidOp
  /-- `String(x)`. -/
  | toStr
  /-- `BigInt(x)`: the bigint of a number whose value is an exact integer, and the
      identity on a bigint.  This is how a JavaScript *number* — the length of an
      array, the result of `indexOf` — is brought into the bigint world the compiled
      `Nat` and `Int` live in.  On anything else it is stuck: `BigInt(1.5)` is a
      `RangeError`, and the string and boolean conversions JavaScript also allows are
      deliberately not modelled. -/
  | toBigInt
  /-- `s.toUpperCase()`. -/
  | strUpper
  /-- `s.toLowerCase()`. -/
  | strLower
  /-- `s.trim()`. -/
  | strTrim
  /-- `s.trimStart()`. -/
  | strTrimStart
  /-- `x.toFixed(6)`: the six-decimal form of a number, as a string, with
      JavaScript's spelling of the special values (`NaN`, `Infinity`,
      `-Infinity`).  This is the second node `EXPR-DROP-LAM-CALL-PLAN.md` §2.5 asks
      for: printing a `Float` used to be hidden from the optimizer behind an
      immediately invoked arrow, because the receiver of a method call had to be kept
      away from a value model that has no methods.  As an operation it simply folds
      when the number is known. -/
  | toFixed6
  /-- `s.trimEnd()`. -/
  | strTrimEnd
  /-- `Object.keys(o)`. -/
  | objKeys
  /-- `Object.values(o)`. -/
  | objValues
  /-- `Object.entries(o)`. -/
  | objEntries
  deriving Repr, DecidableEq, BEq, Inhabited

/-- The strings that name an opaque global: a non-empty string. -/
abbrev NEString := NonEmptyString

mutual

/-- Exactly `k` expressions: the fields of a compiled constructor. -/
inductive ExprVec : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Nat → Type where
  | nil  : ExprVec n js g 0
  | cons (head : Expr n js g) (tail : ExprVec n js g k) : ExprVec n js g (k + 1)

/-- A compiled Lean constructor application, `{ tag: "cons", _1: …, _2: … }`.
    It is indexed by a *recursion-aware* family, so a fold over it terminates. -/
inductive LeanEnum : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Type where
  | mk (fam : LeanRecFamily) (ctor : LeanRecCtorSchema)
       (fields : ExprVec n js g ctor.numFields)
       (h_mem : fam.allCtors.contains ctor = true) : LeanEnum n js g

/-- The properties of an object literal: a static `key: value` pair, a computed
    `[key]: value` pair, or a `...src` spread of another object.

    The three shapes are separate constructors rather than one `cons` with a key
    that is either a string or an expression: the encoding is the same size, but it
    leaves every existing static-key case of every pass untouched. -/
inductive ObjProps : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Type where
  | nil : ObjProps n js g
  | cons (prop : NonEmptyString) (val : Expr n js g) (tail : ObjProps n js g) : ObjProps n js g
  /-- `{[key]: val, …}`: the key is `String(key)`, computed at evaluation time. -/
  | computed (key : Expr n js g) (val : Expr n js g) (tail : ObjProps n js g) : ObjProps n js g
  /-- `{...src, …}`: the own enumerable properties of `src`, spliced in at this
      position.  Merging is left to right: a later key overwrites an earlier one's
      *value* but not its *position*, which is exactly what property lookup and
      `Object.keys` on a list with duplicate keys already do. -/
  | spread (src : Expr n js g) (tail : ObjProps n js g) : ObjProps n js g

/-- The elements of an array literal; an element is an item or a spread. -/
inductive ExprList : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Type where
  | nil : ExprList n js g
  | cons   (head : Expr n js g) (tail : ExprList n js g) : ExprList n js g
  | spread (head : Expr n js g) (tail : ExprList n js g) : ExprList n js g

/--
The branches of a `foldEnum` over `fam`.

This is where the *strictness* of `LeanRecFamily` pays off: a branch may only mention
a constructor that really belongs to the family (`h_mem`), and its body binds exactly
`ctor.numFields` variables — the constructor's fields, with each **recursive** field
replaced by the already-folded result of that sub-value.  Neither an unknown tag nor
a wrong arity is representable.
-/
inductive Branches : LeanRecFamily → Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Type where
  | nil : Branches fam n js g
  | cons (ctor : LeanRecCtorSchema) (h_mem : fam.allCtors.contains ctor = true)
         (body : Expr (n + ctor.numFields) js g) (tail : Branches fam n js g) : Branches fam n js g

/--
The body of a `loop`: an expression in **tail position** with respect to a loop of
`a` loop variables.

This is the plan's `TailExpr` (`EXPR-DROP-LAM-CALL-PLAN.md` §2.2), and the reason it
is a syntactic category of its own rather than "an `Expr` that may say `next`" is
that `next` — go round the loop again — is then in tail position *by construction*:

* a `next` carries an `ExprVec … a`, so going round with the wrong number of values
  is not a term;
* there is no node that puts a `TailExpr` under an operator, so `1 + next …` cannot
  be written, and the `while` loop with an assignment to the loop variables that
  `LakeJs.Render` emits is a correct compilation of every `TailExpr` there is;
* the recursion of the language is confined to this one place: an `Expr` under a
  `ret`, an `ite` condition or a `bind` right-hand side is an ordinary `Expr`, and
  the optimizer descends into it like it does into any other subterm.

The label context is the *same* `js` as the enclosing expression's: a loop body may
jump to a join point outside the loop (which is a `break` out of it), and, more
prosaically, the substitutions of `LakeJs.Stage` map variables to `Expr n js g`, so
a body in a different label context could not be substituted into.  A `case` in tail
position is `ite` on `BExpr.isTag` with the fields bound by `bind`; the four nodes
below are exactly those of the miniature language of `LakeJs.Ext.Labels`, where the
loop's metatheory was settled before it was added here. -/
inductive TailExpr : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Nat → Type where
  /-- Leave the loop with this value. -/
  | ret  (e : Expr n js g)                                  : TailExpr n js g a
  /-- Go round again with these values of the loop variables. -/
  | next (args : ExprVec n js g a)                          : TailExpr n js g a
  /-- Test, and continue in tail position on both sides. -/
  | ite  (c : BExpr n js g) (t e : TailExpr n js g a)       : TailExpr n js g a
  /-- Name a value, and continue in tail position.  The bound variable is `#n`, as
      for `Expr.letIn`. -/
  | bind (rhs : Expr n js g) (k : TailExpr (n + 1) js g a)   : TailExpr n js g a

/-- Expressions statically known to evaluate to a JavaScript boolean. -/
inductive BExpr : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Type where
  | tt                                          : BExpr n js g
  | ff                                          : BExpr n js g
  | truthy  (e : Expr n js g)                       : BExpr n js g
  | not     (c : BExpr n js g)                      : BExpr n js g
  | and     (c d : BExpr n js g)                    : BExpr n js g
  | or      (c d : BExpr n js g)                    : BExpr n js g
  | binop   (op : BinOpBool) (a b : Expr n js g)   : BExpr n js g
  /-- `Array.isArray(e)`. -/
  | isArray (e : Expr n js g)                       : BExpr n js g
  /-- `e.tag === "ctor"`. -/
  | isTag   (obj : Expr n js g) (tag : NonEmptyString) : BExpr n js g

/-- A terminating JavaScript expression with `n` free de Bruijn variables. -/
inductive Expr : Nat → List Nat → Finset NEString → Type where
  | var          (i : Fin n)                          : Expr n js g
  /-- An opaque name emitted as-is.  It is a *value*, not something that can be
      called — there is no application node. -/
  | unsafeGlobal (name : NEString) (mem : name ∈ g)   : Expr n js g
  | bexpr        (b : BExpr n js g)                       : Expr n js g
  /-- **The** numeric literal of the language.  It holds the number itself, in the
      one exact model of `LakeJs.JSNumber` — an exact rational, `NaN`, `±Infinity`
      or `-0` — and not the source text of a literal, so every literal denotes a
      definite JavaScript number.  There is no second, `Nat`-shaped constructor:
      `Expr.num k` (below) is an abbreviation for `.numLit (JSNumber.ofNat k)`. -/
  | numLit       (val : JSNumber)                     : Expr n js g
  /-- **The** bigint literal, `1n`.  A bigint is a JavaScript type of its own — it is
      never `===` to a number, and mixing the two in arithmetic is a `TypeError` —
      so it is a constructor of its own, holding an exact `Int`.  Its operator layer
      is `LakeJs.JSBigInt`. -/
  | bigintLit    (val : Int)                          : Expr n js g
  | str          (val : String)                       : Expr n js g
  | undefined                                         : Expr n js g
  | null                                              : Expr n js g
  | obj          (props : ObjProps n js g)                : Expr n js g
  | arr          (elems : ExprList n js g)                : Expr n js g
  | prop         (obj : Expr n js g) (name : NonEmptyString) : Expr n js g
  | index        (obj idx : Expr n js g)                  : Expr n js g
  | unary        (op : UnOp) (a : Expr n js g)           : Expr n js g
  | binop        (op : BinOp) (a b : Expr n js g)        : Expr n js g
  | cond         (c : BExpr n js g) (t e : Expr n js g)       : Expr n js g
  /-- An applied Lean constructor. -/
  | leanEnum     (e : LeanEnum n js g)                    : Expr n js g
  /-- `obj._{idx+1}`: the `idx`-th field of a compiled Lean constructor. -/
  | getField     (obj : Expr n js g) (idx : Nat)          : Expr n js g
  /-- The catamorphism over a value of `fam`.  `dflt` is the result when the value's
      tag has no branch. -/
  | foldEnum     (fam : LeanRecFamily) (scrut : Expr n js g) (branches : Branches fam n js g)
                 (dflt : Expr n js g)                     : Expr n js g
  /-- `arr.reduceRight((acc, item) => body, init)` (or `reduce`).  The body binds two
      variables: `acc` is `#n` and `item` is `#(n+1)`. -/
  | foldArray    (dir : FoldDir) (arr : Expr n js g) (init : Expr n js g)
                 (body : Expr (n + 2) js g)               : Expr n js g
  /-- `let`: evaluate `rhs`, then evaluate `body` with the result bound to the fresh
      variable `#n`.  Rendered as the IIFE `((_lN) => body)(rhs)`, which is the only
      way an *expression* grammar can name a value.  It is the language's one
      non-fold binder, and it adds no new shape of recursion: `body` is a strict
      subterm, so every traversal stays structural. -/
  | letIn        (rhs : Expr n js g) (body : Expr (n + 1) js g) : Expr n js g
  /-- A **join point**: a local continuation of `arity` parameters that the
      continuation `k` may jump to with `jmp`.  This is LCNF's `Code.jp`, and it is
      what a `case` whose branches share a tail compiles to.

      Two facts are read off the type rather than checked:

      * `k` is typed in `arity :: js`, so the label is in scope **only** in the
        continuation — a join point cannot jump to itself, and the label context
        grows exactly like the variable context does under a binder;
      * a `jmp` carries an `ExprVec` of length `js[i]`, so a jump with the wrong
        number of arguments is not representable.

      Together they are what keeps the language terminating with join points in it:
      the body of a `jp` lives in a strictly smaller label context than the jump
      that reaches it. -/
  | jp           (arity : Nat) (paramTys : List Ty) (body : Expr (n + arity) js g)
                 (k : Expr n (arity :: js) g)          : Expr n js g
  /-- A jump to the `i`-th enclosing join point, with exactly `js[i]` arguments. -/
  | jmp          (i : Fin js.length) (args : ExprVec n js g js[i]) : Expr n js g
  /-- A **function value**, `(p₀, …, p_{arity-1}) => body`.  The parameters occupy
      the last `arity` de Bruijn indices of `body`, exactly as the two parameters of
      a `foldArray` body do.

      A `funVal` is *data* as far as this language is concerned: the optimizer
      descends into `body` like it does into any other subterm, and **no rule ever
      applies one** — see `extApply` below.  It is nevertheless not an
      `Expr.isValue`: the values of this development are the ones the folds inline
      and `Expr.jsStr?` can print, and a function is neither, so a `funVal` stays
      residual exactly as a `jp` or an `extApply` does. -/
  | funVal       (arity : Nat) (body : Expr (n + arity) js g) : Expr n js g
  /-- Application of a function value that came from **outside** the term — a
      parameter of arrow type, a field of a dictionary, the result of another
      external application: `fn(a₀, …)`.  The arguments are an `ExprList`, so
      `f(...xs)` is expressible too.

      The engine has **no β rule**: it never contracts `extApply (funVal k b) as`,
      and `Expr.run` answers `Value.opaque` for both nodes.  That is what keeps the
      language total — `LakeJs.Ext.Lambda` shows that a λ-calculus *with* β has no
      fuel-free interpreter, and the reason that argument does not apply here is
      precisely that this language can build and pass a function but never apply
      one.  β-reduction happens in the **backend**, at compile time, on LCNF, where
      the call graph is known and recursion is detected (`LakeJs.Backend`); what is
      left as an `extApply` is a call to a function that came from outside, which no
      amount of evaluation could resolve.

      Unlike the `call` node it replaces, `extApply` is **not inert**: the engine
      optimizes `fn` and `args`, so nothing in the term escapes the one optimizer. -/
  | extApply     (fn : Expr n js g) (args : ExprList n js g) : Expr n js g
  /-- A **loop**: `arity` loop variables, started from `init`, and a body in tail
      position that either leaves the loop (`TailExpr.ret`) or goes round again
      (`TailExpr.next`).  The loop variables occupy the last `arity` de Bruijn
      indices of the body, exactly as the parameters of a `jp` or a `funVal` do.

      This is the language's only recursive node, and the only one whose meaning is
      not a terminating computation: `Expr.run` answers `Value.opaque "loop"` for it,
      as it does for a `jmp` or an external application, because a loop is faithful
      to the source rather than independently proved terminating
      (`EXPR-DROP-LAM-CALL-PLAN.md` §7, risk 9; `LakeJs.Ext.Labels` measures exactly
      what is lost).  Everything *else* is unchanged: the optimizer's traversal is
      still structural — `init` and `body` are strict subterms — so `Expr.optimize`
      remains a total function, and it is the absence of an unrolling rule, not any
      property of the loop, that keeps it terminating. -/
  | loop         (arity : Nat) (varTys : List Ty) (init : ExprVec n js g arity)
                 (body : TailExpr (n + arity) js g arity)  : Expr n js g

end

-- `js`, the label context, is auto-bound per declaration: the traversals below
-- recurse into the continuation of a `jp`, which lives in a *larger* label context,
-- so `js` must be generalized in each definition rather than fixed by a `variable`.

/-- A non-negative integer literal, `Expr.num k`.  This used to be a *constructor*,
    holding a `Nat`; the numeric port left it as a mere spelling of the one numeric
    literal, so that a number in the object language has exactly one representation
    and the case splits of every function on `Expr` mention it exactly once.  It is
    an `abbrev`, so `Expr.num k` and `Expr.numLit (JSNumber.ofNat k)` are the same
    term and every lemma about `numLit` applies to it. -/
def Expr.num {n : Nat} {g : Finset NEString} (val : Nat) : Expr n js g :=
  .numLit (JSNumber.ofNat val)

/-- `Expr.num` is a spelling, not a node: it unfolds to the one numeric literal, and
    it is a `simp` lemma so that every rule about `numLit` applies to it. -/
@[simp] theorem Expr.num_eq_numLit {n : Nat} {g : Finset NEString} (k : Nat) :
    (Expr.num k : Expr n js g) = .numLit (JSNumber.ofNat k) := rfl

instance : Inhabited (Expr n js g)      := ⟨.undefined⟩
instance : Inhabited (BExpr n js g)     := ⟨.ff⟩
instance : Inhabited (ExprList n js g)  := ⟨.nil⟩
instance : Inhabited (ObjProps n js g)  := ⟨.nil⟩
instance : Inhabited (ExprVec n js g 0) := ⟨.nil⟩

/-! ## Weakening

Declaring more globals than a term uses is always allowed. -/

mutual

/-- Widen the declared globals of an expression. -/
def Expr.mono {n : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') : Expr n js g → Expr n js g'
  | .var i             => .var i
  | .unsafeGlobal s m  => .unsafeGlobal s (h m)
  | .bexpr b           => .bexpr (BExpr.mono h b)
  | .numLit v          => .numLit v
  | .bigintLit v       => .bigintLit v
  | .str v             => .str v
  | .undefined         => .undefined
  | .null              => .null
  | .obj ps            => .obj (ObjProps.mono h ps)
  | .arr es            => .arr (ExprList.mono h es)
  | .prop o p          => .prop (Expr.mono h o) p
  | .index o k         => .index (Expr.mono h o) (Expr.mono h k)
  | .unary op a        => .unary op (Expr.mono h a)
  | .binop op a b      => .binop op (Expr.mono h a) (Expr.mono h b)
  | .cond c t e        => .cond (BExpr.mono h c) (Expr.mono h t) (Expr.mono h e)
  | .leanEnum e        => .leanEnum (LeanEnum.mono h e)
  | .getField o i      => .getField (Expr.mono h o) i
  | .foldEnum fam s bs d =>
      .foldEnum fam (Expr.mono h s) (Branches.mono h bs) (Expr.mono h d)
  | .foldArray d a i b =>
      .foldArray d (Expr.mono h a) (Expr.mono h i) (Expr.mono h b)
  | .letIn r b         => .letIn (Expr.mono h r) (Expr.mono h b)
  | .jp a tys b k        => .jp a tys (Expr.mono h b) (Expr.mono h k)
  | .jmp i as          => .jmp i (ExprVec.mono h as)
  | .funVal k b           => .funVal k (Expr.mono h b)
  | .extApply f as         => .extApply (Expr.mono h f) (ExprList.mono h as)
  | .loop a tys i b    => .loop a tys (ExprVec.mono h i) (TailExpr.mono h b)

/-- Widen the declared globals of a loop body. -/
def TailExpr.mono {n a : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') :
    TailExpr n js g a → TailExpr n js g' a
  | .ret e      => .ret (Expr.mono h e)
  | .next as    => .next (ExprVec.mono h as)
  | .ite c t e  => .ite (BExpr.mono h c) (TailExpr.mono h t) (TailExpr.mono h e)
  | .bind r k   => .bind (Expr.mono h r) (TailExpr.mono h k)

/-- Widen the declared globals of a boolean expression. -/
def BExpr.mono {n : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') : BExpr n js g → BExpr n js g'
  | .tt           => .tt
  | .ff           => .ff
  | .truthy e     => .truthy (Expr.mono h e)
  | .not c        => .not (BExpr.mono h c)
  | .and c d      => .and (BExpr.mono h c) (BExpr.mono h d)
  | .or c d       => .or (BExpr.mono h c) (BExpr.mono h d)
  | .binop op a b => .binop op (Expr.mono h a) (Expr.mono h b)
  | .isArray e    => .isArray (Expr.mono h e)
  | .isTag o t    => .isTag (Expr.mono h o) t

/-- Widen the declared globals of an array literal. -/
def ExprList.mono {n : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') :
    ExprList n js g → ExprList n js g'
  | .nil        => .nil
  | .cons e t   => .cons (Expr.mono h e) (ExprList.mono h t)
  | .spread e t => .spread (Expr.mono h e) (ExprList.mono h t)

/-- Widen the declared globals of an object literal. -/
def ObjProps.mono {n : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') :
    ObjProps n js g → ObjProps n js g'
  | .nil            => .nil
  | .cons p v t     => .cons p (Expr.mono h v) (ObjProps.mono h t)
  | .computed k v t => .computed (Expr.mono h k) (Expr.mono h v) (ObjProps.mono h t)
  | .spread s t     => .spread (Expr.mono h s) (ObjProps.mono h t)

/-- Widen the declared globals of a field vector. -/
def ExprVec.mono {n k : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') :
    ExprVec n js g k → ExprVec n js g' k
  | .nil      => .nil
  | .cons e t => .cons (Expr.mono h e) (ExprVec.mono h t)

/-- Widen the declared globals of a compiled constructor. -/
def LeanEnum.mono {n : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') :
    LeanEnum n js g → LeanEnum n js g'
  | .mk fam c fs hm => .mk fam c (ExprVec.mono h fs) hm

/-- Widen the declared globals of a list of branches. -/
def Branches.mono {fam : LeanRecFamily} {n : Nat} {g g' : Finset NEString} (h : g ⊆ g') :
    Branches fam n js g → Branches fam n js g'
  | .nil            => .nil
  | .cons c hm b t  => .cons c hm (Expr.mono h b) (Branches.mono h t)

end


/-! ## Size

Sizes are only used as the termination measure of the well-founded definitions in
`LakeJs.Eval`; every subterm is strictly smaller than its parent. -/

mutual

def Expr.size {n : Nat} : Expr n js g → Nat
  | .var _ | .unsafeGlobal _ _ | .numLit _ | .bigintLit _ | .str _ | .undefined | .null => 1
  | .bexpr b            => 1 + BExpr.size b
  | .obj props          => 1 + ObjProps.size props
  | .arr elems          => 1 + ExprList.size elems
  | .prop o _           => 1 + Expr.size o
  | .index o i          => 1 + Expr.size o + Expr.size i
  | .unary _ a          => 1 + Expr.size a
  | .binop _ a b        => 1 + Expr.size a + Expr.size b
  | .cond c t e         => 1 + BExpr.size c + Expr.size t + Expr.size e
  | .leanEnum e         => 1 + LeanEnum.size e
  | .getField o _       => 1 + Expr.size o
  | .foldEnum _ s bs d  => 1 + Expr.size s + Branches.size bs + Expr.size d
  | .foldArray _ a i b  => 1 + Expr.size a + Expr.size i + Expr.size b
  | .letIn r b          => 1 + Expr.size r + Expr.size b
  | .jp _ _ b k           => 1 + Expr.size b + Expr.size k
  | .jmp _ as           => 1 + ExprVec.size as
  | .funVal _ b            => 1 + Expr.size b
  | .extApply f as          => 1 + Expr.size f + ExprList.size as
  | .loop _ _ i b       => 1 + ExprVec.size i + TailExpr.size b

def TailExpr.size {n a : Nat} : TailExpr n js g a → Nat
  | .ret e      => 1 + Expr.size e
  | .next as    => 1 + ExprVec.size as
  | .ite c t e  => 1 + BExpr.size c + TailExpr.size t + TailExpr.size e
  | .bind r k   => 1 + Expr.size r + TailExpr.size k

def BExpr.size {n : Nat} : BExpr n js g → Nat
  | .tt | .ff      => 1
  | .truthy e      => 1 + Expr.size e
  | .not c         => 1 + BExpr.size c
  | .and c d       => 1 + BExpr.size c + BExpr.size d
  | .or c d        => 1 + BExpr.size c + BExpr.size d
  | .binop _ a b   => 1 + Expr.size a + Expr.size b
  | .isArray e     => 1 + Expr.size e
  | .isTag o _     => 1 + Expr.size o

def ExprList.size {n : Nat} : ExprList n js g → Nat
  | .nil        => 1
  | .cons e t   => 1 + Expr.size e + ExprList.size t
  | .spread e t => 1 + Expr.size e + ExprList.size t

def ObjProps.size {n : Nat} : ObjProps n js g → Nat
  | .nil            => 1
  | .cons _ v t     => 1 + Expr.size v + ObjProps.size t
  | .computed k v t => 1 + Expr.size k + Expr.size v + ObjProps.size t
  | .spread s t     => 1 + Expr.size s + ObjProps.size t

def ExprVec.size {n k : Nat} : ExprVec n js g k → Nat
  | .nil      => 1
  | .cons e t => 1 + Expr.size e + ExprVec.size t

def LeanEnum.size {n : Nat} : LeanEnum n js g → Nat
  | .mk _ _ f _ => 1 + ExprVec.size f

def Branches.size {fam : LeanRecFamily} {n : Nat} : Branches fam n js g → Nat
  | .nil            => 1
  | .cons _ _ b t   => 1 + Expr.size b + Branches.size t

end

mutual
theorem Expr.size_pos {n : Nat} (e : Expr n js g) : 0 < e.size := by
  cases e <;> simp [Expr.size]

theorem ExprVec.size_pos {n k : Nat} (v : ExprVec n js g k) : 0 < v.size := by
  cases v <;> simp +arith [ExprVec.size]

theorem TailExpr.size_pos {n a : Nat} (t : TailExpr n js g a) : 0 < t.size := by
  cases t <;> simp +arith [TailExpr.size]
end

/-! ## Conversions for the container types -/

/-- One element of an array literal: an item, or a spread. -/
structure ArrElem (n : Nat) (js : List Nat) g where
  isSpread : Bool
  value    : Expr n js g
  deriving Inhabited

namespace ExprList

def toList : ExprList n js g → List (ArrElem n js g)
  | .nil        => []
  | .cons h t   => ⟨false, h⟩ :: t.toList
  | .spread h t => ⟨true, h⟩ :: t.toList

def ofList : List (ArrElem n js g) → ExprList n js g
  | []      => .nil
  | e :: es => if e.isSpread then .spread e.value (ofList es) else .cons e.value (ofList es)

/-- Build a spread-free list of items. -/
def ofItems : List (Expr n js g) → ExprList n js g
  | []      => .nil
  | e :: es => .cons e (ofItems es)

@[simp] theorem ofList_toList : ∀ l : ExprList n js g, ofList l.toList = l
  | .nil        => rfl
  | .cons h t   => by simp [toList, ofList, ofList_toList t]
  | .spread h t => by simp [toList, ofList, ofList_toList t]

/-- Are all elements plain items (so the length is statically known)? -/
def hasNoSpread : ExprList n js g → Bool
  | .nil        => true
  | .cons _ t   => t.hasNoSpread
  | .spread _ _ => false

def length : ExprList n js g → Nat
  | .nil        => 0
  | .cons _ t   => t.length + 1
  | .spread _ t => t.length + 1

/-- Append two array literals. -/
def append : ExprList n js g → ExprList n js g → ExprList n js g
  | .nil,        r => r
  | .cons h t,   r => .cons h (append t r)
  | .spread h t, r => .spread h (append t r)

/-- The `i`-th element, provided everything before it is a plain item. -/
def get? : ExprList n js g → Nat → Option (Expr n js g)
  | .nil,        _     => none
  | .cons h _,   0     => some h
  | .cons _ t,   i + 1 => t.get? i
  | .spread _ _, _     => none

end ExprList

namespace ExprVec

def toList : ExprVec n js g k → List (Expr n js g)
  | .nil      => []
  | .cons h t => h :: t.toList

def ofList : (l : List (Expr n js g)) → ExprVec n js g l.length
  | []      => .nil
  | e :: es => .cons e (ofList es)

def map (f : Expr n js g → Expr m js g) : ExprVec n js g k → ExprVec m js g k
  | .nil      => .nil
  | .cons h t => .cons (f h) (map f t)

def cast (h : k = k') (v : ExprVec n js g k) : ExprVec n js g k' := h ▸ v

@[simp] theorem toList_length : ∀ v : ExprVec n js g k, v.toList.length = k
  | .nil      => rfl
  | .cons h t => by simp [toList, toList_length t]

def get? : ExprVec n js g k → Nat → Option (Expr n js g)
  | .nil,      _     => none
  | .cons h _, 0     => some h
  | .cons _ t, i + 1 => t.get? i

/-- Build a vector of the required length from a list, padding with `undefined`. -/
def ofListPad : (k : Nat) → List (Expr n js g) → ExprVec n js g k
  | 0,     _       => .nil
  | k + 1, []      => .cons .undefined (ofListPad k [])
  | k + 1, e :: es => .cons e (ofListPad k es)

end ExprVec

namespace ObjProps

/-- Has this property list only static `key: value` pairs?  Every rule that reads
    the keys of an object literal off its syntax is guarded by this: a computed key
    or a spread of an unresolved source hides both which keys the literal has and
    what they are worth. -/
def isStatic : ObjProps n js g → Bool
  | .nil            => true
  | .cons _ _ t     => t.isStatic
  | .computed _ _ _ => false
  | .spread _ _     => false

/-- The static pairs, in order; a computed key or a spread ends the list.  Only
    meaningful on an `isStatic` list. -/
def toList : ObjProps n js g → List (NonEmptyString × Expr n js g)
  | .nil            => []
  | .cons p v tail  => (p, v) :: tail.toList
  | .computed _ _ _ => []
  | .spread _ _     => []

def ofList : List (NonEmptyString × Expr n js g) → ObjProps n js g
  | []             => .nil
  | (p, v) :: tail => .cons p v (ofList tail)

/-- Append two property lists, left to right. -/
def append : ObjProps n js g → ObjProps n js g → ObjProps n js g
  | .nil,            r => r
  | .cons p v t,     r => .cons p v (append t r)
  | .computed k v t, r => .computed k v (append t r)
  | .spread s t,     r => .spread s (append t r)

/-- Look up a property; later duplicates shadow earlier ones, as in JavaScript.
    On a list that is not `isStatic` the answer is not the property's value — a
    spread may supply or overwrite it — so every caller guards on `isStatic`. -/
def find? : ObjProps n js g → NonEmptyString → Option (Expr n js g)
  | .nil,        _ => none
  | .cons p v t, k => match t.find? k with
                      | some v' => some v'
                      | none    => if p == k then some v else none
  | .computed _ _ _, _ => none
  | .spread _ _,     _ => none

end ObjProps

/-! ## `LeanEnum` accessors -/

namespace LeanEnum

def fam : LeanEnum n js g → LeanRecFamily
  | .mk f _ _ _ => f

def ctor : LeanEnum n js g → LeanRecCtorSchema
  | .mk _ c _ _ => c

def tag (e : LeanEnum n js g) : String := e.ctor.name

def fields : (e : LeanEnum n js g) → ExprVec n js g e.ctor.numFields
  | .mk _ _ f _ => f

theorem h_mem : (e : LeanEnum n js g) → e.fam.allCtors.contains e.ctor = true
  | .mk _ _ _ h => h

/-- The constructor of a value really belongs to its family. -/
theorem ctor_mem (e : LeanEnum n js g) : e.ctor ∈ e.fam.allCtors :=
  LeanRecFamily.mem_of_contains e.h_mem

/-- Looking the value's tag up in its family returns the value's own constructor —
    the invariant that lets a fold recover the field kinds from a tag. -/
theorem findCtor?_tag (e : LeanEnum n js g) : e.fam.findCtor? e.tag = some e.ctor :=
  LeanRecFamily.findCtor?_of_mem e.ctor_mem

def fieldList (e : LeanEnum n js g) : List (Expr n js g) := e.fields.toList

@[simp] theorem fieldList_length (e : LeanEnum n js g) : e.fieldList.length = e.ctor.numFields := by
  simp [fieldList]

end LeanEnum

/-- The arity found by tag lookup is the arity of the constructor found. -/
theorem LeanRecFamily.ctorNumFields_getCtor (fam : LeanRecFamily) (tag : String)
    (h : fam.hasCtor tag) : fam.ctorNumFields tag = (fam.getCtor tag h).numFields := by
  have hfind : fam.findCtor? tag = some (fam.getCtor tag h) := (Option.some_get h).symm
  simp [LeanRecFamily.ctorNumFields, hfind]

/-- A tag of the family really names one of its constructors. -/
theorem LeanRecFamily.getCtor_mem (fam : LeanRecFamily) (tag : String) (h : fam.hasCtor tag) :
    fam.allCtors.contains (fam.getCtor tag h) = true := by
  have hfind : fam.findCtor? tag = some (fam.getCtor tag h) := (Option.some_get h).symm
  have hmem : fam.getCtor tag h ∈ fam.allCtors :=
    List.mem_of_find?_eq_some (by simpa [LeanRecFamily.findCtor?] using hfind)
  exact List.contains_iff_mem.mpr hmem

/-- Smart constructor: build a compiled constructor value from a tag.  The arity is
    forced by the type of `fields`, and membership in the family is discharged by
    `decide` on the concrete literals. -/
def LeanRecFamily.mkEnum (fam : LeanRecFamily) (tag : String)
    (fields : ExprVec n js g (fam.ctorNumFields tag))
    (h : fam.hasCtor tag := by decide) : LeanEnum n js g :=
  .mk fam (fam.getCtor tag h)
    (ExprVec.cast (fam.ctorNumFields_getCtor tag h) fields)
    (fam.getCtor_mem tag h)

/-- The `Expr` node for an applied Lean constructor, by tag. -/
def Expr.mkEnum (fam : LeanRecFamily) (tag : String)
    (fields : ExprVec n js g (fam.ctorNumFields tag))
    (h : fam.hasCtor tag := by decide) : Expr n js g :=
  .leanEnum (fam.mkEnum tag fields h)

/-- A branch of a `foldEnum`, by tag: the body binds the constructor's fields, with
    the recursive ones already folded. -/
def Branches.consTag (fam : LeanRecFamily) (tag : String)
    (body : Expr (n + fam.ctorNumFields tag) js g) (tail : Branches fam n js g)
    (h : fam.hasCtor tag := by decide) : Branches fam n js g :=
  .cons (fam.getCtor tag h) (fam.getCtor_mem tag h)
    (fam.ctorNumFields_getCtor tag h ▸ body) tail

/-! ## Occurrence analysis

Does an expression use a given free variable?  The parameters of a binder occupy the
*last* indices, so an index that is free in the whole expression keeps its number
inside a nested binder and no shifting is needed. -/

mutual

def Expr.mentions (i : Nat) : Expr n js g → Bool
  | .var j            => j.val == i
  | .unsafeGlobal _ _ | .numLit _ | .bigintLit _ | .str _ | .undefined | .null => false
  | .bexpr b          => BExpr.mentions i b
  | .obj props        => ObjProps.mentions i props
  | .arr elems        => ExprList.mentions i elems
  | .prop o _         => Expr.mentions i o
  | .index o k        => Expr.mentions i o || Expr.mentions i k
  | .unary _ a        => Expr.mentions i a
  | .binop _ a b      => Expr.mentions i a || Expr.mentions i b
  | .cond c t e       => BExpr.mentions i c || Expr.mentions i t || Expr.mentions i e
  | .leanEnum e       => LeanEnum.mentions i e
  | .getField o _     => Expr.mentions i o
  | .foldEnum _ s bs d =>
      Expr.mentions i s || Branches.mentions i bs || Expr.mentions i d
  | .foldArray _ a k b =>
      Expr.mentions i a || Expr.mentions i k || Expr.mentions i b
  -- The binder's parameter occupies the *last* index, so an index that is free in
  -- the whole term keeps its number inside the body and needs no shifting.
  | .letIn r b        => Expr.mentions i r || Expr.mentions i b
  | .jp _ _ b k         => Expr.mentions i b || Expr.mentions i k
  | .jmp _ as         => ExprVec.mentions i as
  | .funVal _ b          => Expr.mentions i b
  | .extApply f as        => Expr.mentions i f || ExprList.mentions i as
  | .loop _ _ ini b   => ExprVec.mentions i ini || TailExpr.mentions i b

def TailExpr.mentions (i : Nat) {a : Nat} : TailExpr n js g a → Bool
  | .ret e      => Expr.mentions i e
  | .next as    => ExprVec.mentions i as
  | .ite c t e  => BExpr.mentions i c || TailExpr.mentions i t || TailExpr.mentions i e
  | .bind r k   => Expr.mentions i r || TailExpr.mentions i k

def BExpr.mentions (i : Nat) : BExpr n js g → Bool
  | .tt | .ff     => false
  | .truthy e     => Expr.mentions i e
  | .not c        => BExpr.mentions i c
  | .and c d      => BExpr.mentions i c || BExpr.mentions i d
  | .or c d       => BExpr.mentions i c || BExpr.mentions i d
  | .binop _ a b  => Expr.mentions i a || Expr.mentions i b
  | .isArray e    => Expr.mentions i e
  | .isTag o _    => Expr.mentions i o

def ExprList.mentions (i : Nat) : ExprList n js g → Bool
  | .nil        => false
  | .cons h t   => Expr.mentions i h || ExprList.mentions i t
  | .spread h t => Expr.mentions i h || ExprList.mentions i t

def ObjProps.mentions (i : Nat) : ObjProps n js g → Bool
  | .nil            => false
  | .cons _ v t     => Expr.mentions i v || ObjProps.mentions i t
  | .computed k v t => Expr.mentions i k || Expr.mentions i v || ObjProps.mentions i t
  | .spread s t     => Expr.mentions i s || ObjProps.mentions i t

def ExprVec.mentions (i : Nat) {k : Nat} : ExprVec n js g k → Bool
  | .nil      => false
  | .cons h t => Expr.mentions i h || ExprVec.mentions i t

def LeanEnum.mentions (i : Nat) : LeanEnum n js g → Bool
  | .mk _ _ fields _ => ExprVec.mentions i fields

def Branches.mentions {fam : LeanRecFamily} (i : Nat) : Branches fam n js g → Bool
  | .nil             => false
  | .cons _ _ body t => Expr.mentions i body || Branches.mentions i t

end

/-! ## Counting occurrences

`Expr.mentions` answers *whether* a variable is used; the rewrite rule that inlines a
binding needs to know *how often*.  Two conventions make the count a measure of the
**cost of duplicating** the variable's value rather than of the number of syntactic
occurrences:

* an occurrence under a binder — a fold's body, a branch of a `foldEnum` — counts
  **twice**, because the body runs once per element of the array or per node of the
  value, so duplicating a term into it duplicates work rather than code;
* both arms of a `cond` count, even though only one of them runs.

The only thing the two notions have to agree on is zero: a variable is mentioned
exactly when it occurs at least once (`Expr.mentions_iff_occurrences_pos`). -/

mutual

def Expr.occurrences (i : Nat) : Expr n js g → Nat
  | .var j            => if j.val == i then 1 else 0
  | .unsafeGlobal _ _ | .numLit _ | .bigintLit _ | .str _ | .undefined | .null => 0
  | .bexpr b          => BExpr.occurrences i b
  | .obj props        => ObjProps.occurrences i props
  | .arr elems        => ExprList.occurrences i elems
  | .prop o _         => Expr.occurrences i o
  | .index o k        => Expr.occurrences i o + Expr.occurrences i k
  | .unary _ a        => Expr.occurrences i a
  | .binop _ a b      => Expr.occurrences i a + Expr.occurrences i b
  | .cond c t e       =>
      BExpr.occurrences i c + Expr.occurrences i t + Expr.occurrences i e
  | .leanEnum e       => LeanEnum.occurrences i e
  | .getField o _     => Expr.occurrences i o
  | .foldEnum _ s bs d =>
      Expr.occurrences i s + 2 * Branches.occurrences i bs + Expr.occurrences i d
  | .foldArray _ a k b =>
      Expr.occurrences i a + Expr.occurrences i k + 2 * Expr.occurrences i b
  -- The body of a binding runs exactly once, so its occurrences count once.
  | .letIn r b        => Expr.occurrences i r + Expr.occurrences i b
  -- The body of a join point runs once per *jump*, so — as for a function body —
  -- an occurrence inside it counts twice: duplicating a term into it duplicates
  -- work rather than code.
  | .jp _ _ b k         => 2 * Expr.occurrences i b + Expr.occurrences i k
  | .jmp _ as         => ExprVec.occurrences i as
  -- The body of a function runs once per *call*, so — as for a fold body — an
  -- occurrence inside it counts twice: duplicating a term into it duplicates work.
  | .funVal _ b          => 2 * Expr.occurrences i b
  | .extApply f as        => Expr.occurrences i f + ExprList.occurrences i as
  -- The body of a loop runs once per *iteration*, so an occurrence inside it counts
  -- twice, for the same reason a fold body's does.
  | .loop _ _ ini b   => ExprVec.occurrences i ini + 2 * TailExpr.occurrences i b

def TailExpr.occurrences (i : Nat) {a : Nat} : TailExpr n js g a → Nat
  | .ret e      => Expr.occurrences i e
  | .next as    => ExprVec.occurrences i as
  | .ite c t e  =>
      BExpr.occurrences i c + TailExpr.occurrences i t + TailExpr.occurrences i e
  | .bind r k   => Expr.occurrences i r + TailExpr.occurrences i k

def BExpr.occurrences (i : Nat) : BExpr n js g → Nat
  | .tt | .ff     => 0
  | .truthy e     => Expr.occurrences i e
  | .not c        => BExpr.occurrences i c
  | .and c d      => BExpr.occurrences i c + BExpr.occurrences i d
  | .or c d       => BExpr.occurrences i c + BExpr.occurrences i d
  | .binop _ a b  => Expr.occurrences i a + Expr.occurrences i b
  | .isArray e    => Expr.occurrences i e
  | .isTag o _    => Expr.occurrences i o

def ExprList.occurrences (i : Nat) : ExprList n js g → Nat
  | .nil        => 0
  | .cons h t   => Expr.occurrences i h + ExprList.occurrences i t
  | .spread h t => Expr.occurrences i h + ExprList.occurrences i t

def ObjProps.occurrences (i : Nat) : ObjProps n js g → Nat
  | .nil            => 0
  | .cons _ v t     => Expr.occurrences i v + ObjProps.occurrences i t
  | .computed k v t => Expr.occurrences i k + Expr.occurrences i v + ObjProps.occurrences i t
  | .spread s t     => Expr.occurrences i s + ObjProps.occurrences i t

def ExprVec.occurrences (i : Nat) {k : Nat} : ExprVec n js g k → Nat
  | .nil      => 0
  | .cons h t => Expr.occurrences i h + ExprVec.occurrences i t

def LeanEnum.occurrences (i : Nat) : LeanEnum n js g → Nat
  | .mk _ _ fields _ => ExprVec.occurrences i fields

def Branches.occurrences {fam : LeanRecFamily} (i : Nat) : Branches fam n js g → Nat
  | .nil             => 0
  | .cons _ _ body t => Expr.occurrences i body + Branches.occurrences i t

end

/-! ## Recognising the "build an array" fold

A fold whose recursive branch is `[items…, ...rest]` — where `rest` is the folded
tail and the items do not mention it — is exactly an array being built from the front.
Such a fold does not need the stack that a general catamorphism needs: the items can
be `push`ed while walking the value forwards.  `LakeJs.Render` uses these
predicates to emit that loop. -/

/-- Is this array literal of the form `items…, ...#i` with no item mentioning `#i`? -/
def ExprList.isPushShape (i : Nat) : ExprList n js g → Bool
  | .nil                  => false
  | .spread (.var j) .nil => j.val == i
  | .spread _ _           => false
  | .cons h t             => !Expr.mentions i h && isPushShape i t

namespace Branches

/-- Find the branch for a tag, together with its constructor. -/
def find? {fam : LeanRecFamily} {n : Nat} :
    Branches fam n js g → String →
    Option ((c : LeanRecCtorSchema) × Expr (n + c.numFields) js g)
  | .nil,             _   => none
  | .cons c _ body t, tag => if c.name == tag then some ⟨c, body⟩ else t.find? tag

/-- Is there a branch for this tag? -/
def hasTag {fam : LeanRecFamily} {n : Nat} (tag : String) : Branches fam n js g → Bool
  | .nil             => false
  | .cons c _ _ t    => c.name == tag || hasTag tag t

/-- Does every branch have the shape the array-building loop needs: an array literal,
    which for the recursive constructor ends in a spread of the folded tail? -/
def pushShapeOk {fam : LeanRecFamily} {n : Nat} : Branches fam n js g → Bool
  | .nil             => true
  | .cons c _ body t =>
      (match body with
       | .arr elems =>
           if c.numRecFields == 1 then
             elems.isPushShape (n + (c.recFieldIdxs.headD 0))
           else true
       | _ => false)
      && pushShapeOk t

end Branches

/-- Is this fold an array being built from the front — one recursive constructor,
    a branch for every constructor, and every branch body an array literal whose
    recursive branch ends in the folded tail?  Then it compiles to a `while` loop
    with `push` instead of to a stack-based fold. -/
def Branches.isPushFold {n : Nat} (fam : LeanRecFamily) (bs : Branches fam n js g) : Bool :=
  (fam.allCtors.filter (fun c => c.numRecFields == 1)).length == 1
    && fam.allCtors.all (fun c => bs.hasTag c.name)
    && bs.pushShapeOk

end
